{
  "result": "There's a specific kind of light at 6:42 in the morning that doesn't belong to day or night. It's blue and thin, and it makes everything look honest.\n\nI think about that light a lot, because most of us miss it. We're either still asleep, tangled in yesterday's dreams, or already scrolling, already bracing for the inbox. But if you happen to be awake \u2014 really awake, with your feet on the cold floor \u2014 you catch the world in between performances.\n\nThe coffee maker gurgles like it's clearing its throat. Outside, a single crow makes a decision and then unmakes it. Your house isn't tidy, but it's yours. The unread books on the shelf don't judge you; they wait.\n\nWe spend so much life trying to be impressive. We curate, we optimize, we produce. But there is a quiet rebellion in just noticing. To notice how the steam curls from your mug and disappears before it can be photographed. To notice that your plant has grown two new leaves while you were worrying about something else entirely.\n\nMaybe meaning isn't something you build like a cathedral. Maybe it's something you collect like smooth stones in your pockets. One for today: the early light. One for yesterday: a good text message. One for tomorrow: nothing yet, and that's fine. Plenty of pocket space left.\n\nKeep walking. Keep looking down every once in a while. You'd be surprised what you end up carrying home. That's enough for one blue morning today.",
